---
import Layout from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { base } from "../lib/site";
---
<Layout
  title="Spain"
  description="IU study abroad at Universidad Complutense de Madrid, plus Segovia, Cáceres, Trujillo, and Tenerife ... a student map, not a postcard."
  canonical="https://zhao-langxi.github.io/zhao-langxi/spain.html"
  current="spain"
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <header>
      <p>Study abroad · Spring 2026 · Universidad Complutense de Madrid</p>
      <h1>Spain</h1>
      <p>
        IU can send you further than Indiana. Mine went to Madrid. The part that stuck was not the
        postcard version. It was learning a city that was never designed around you personally, then
        finding a rhythm anyway.
      </p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <section aria-labelledby="ucm-heading">
        <h2 id="ucm-heading">Complutense is not Sample Gates</h2>
        <p>
          Universidad Complutense de Madrid (UCM) sits in Ciudad Universitaria, in the Moncloa area.
          IU's own Madrid handbook is honest about this: it is not a campus in the American sense.
          Fewer organised student activities. More city. Buses and metro connect you to the centre.
        </p>
        <p>
          What you <em>can</em> do, from the
          <a href="https://iu.pressbooks.pub/edabroadmadrid2025/chapter/student-activities/" rel="noopener noreferrer">IU Madrid student-activities chapter</a>:
          join a facultad sports club if you want one (past students have done football, basketball,
          rugby, cheerleading, volleyball). Ask about an <em>intercambio</em> ... a language partner
          paired with a Spanish student. Volunteer through Solidarios or another ONG if you can commit
          the time. Semester students often find the six-month volunteer minimum hard. Ask early.
        </p>
        <p>
          UCM also runs museums, literary week, theatre groups, choirs, and concerts. That is the
          university layer. Most of student life still happens in the city.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="madrid-heading">
        <h2 id="madrid-heading">Madrid, as I actually lived it</h2>
        <p>
          Rooftops at blue hour. Pink drink in a purple bottle on a sunny street. A gym treadmill in a
          new zip code because routines travel too. Spanish homework that says <em>El Español y Yo</em>.
          Puerta del Sol at night, wet stone, flags. A residence courtyard with a pool and a basketball
          court. Film camera in my bag because noticing light still works when the language is harder.
        </p>
        <p>
          The city is the activity. Metro. Walking. A museum day. A quiet balcony. Football on a hostel
          television because it is everywhere. You do not need a club roster to have a semester.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="beyond-heading">
        <h2 id="beyond-heading">Beyond Madrid</h2>
        <p>
          The IU Madrid programme builds in cultural excursions to UNESCO World Heritage sites.
          On Instagram the highlights are the honest index. I am not inventing itineraries I did not live.
        </p>
        <ul class="direction-list">
          <li>
            <h3>Segovia</h3>
            <p>
              A day that looks like terracotta roofs and a bronze devil statue posing like it wants a selfie.
              Close enough for a student trip. Far enough to remember Spain is not only the capital.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Cáceres and Trujillo</h3>
            <p>
              Extremadura. Old stone towns. The kind of excursion a US programme puts on the calendar
              so you leave Madrid on purpose.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Tenerife</h3>
            <p>
              Canary Islands. A longer hop. Sea and volcanic light. Proof that a spring semester can
              hold more than one climate.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="if-you-go-heading">
        <h2 id="if-you-go-heading">If you go</h2>
        <p>
          Apply through IU. Read the handbook before you pack. Housing is apartments, residence halls,
          or homestays ... you arrange it. The city will not flatten itself into a campus loop.
          That is the gift and the work.
        </p>
        <ul class="proof-list">
          <li><a href="https://abroad.iu.edu/" rel="noopener noreferrer">IU Education Abroad</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://iu.pressbooks.pub/edabroadmadrid2025/chapter/wip-madrid-program-goals/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Madrid programme goals</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.instagram.com/zhao.langxi" rel="noopener noreferrer">@zhao.langxi highlights · madrid, segovia, tenerife, caceres, trujillo</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <a href={`${base}iu.html`}>Back to IU</a>
          <a href={`${base}`}>Home</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
